T1  - Five Years Later: contextual Factors and Subjective Experiences Reported by Participants in a Psilocybin for Depression Randomized Clinical Trial

A1  - Della Crosse M
A1  - Morton E
A1  - Sepeda N
A1  - Levin A
A1  - Gilead S
A1  - Cosimano M
A1  - Shaub H
A1  - Washington T
A1  - Gaughan S
A1  - Armstrong S
A1  - Barrett F
A1  - Davis A
Y1  - 2025//
Y2  - 2025//
N2  - Introduction: Depression profoundly impairs psychosocial functioning and quality of life. Many people demonstrate inadequate responses to existing treatments, underscoring the urgent need for innovative approaches. Psilocybin-assisted therapy (PAT) has demonstrated potential for rapid/sustained antidepressant effects, but little is known about factors that predict positive outcomes. This study uniquely explores the contextual factors and subjective experiences that contribute to long-term outcomes, addressing a critical gap in the literature. Method: Using a convergent parallel mixed-methods design, participants (n = 18, 75% of original 24 trial participants) completed self-report measures assessing functional impairment, nature relatedness, and retrospective acute subjective effects (Insight, Challenging Experience, Mystical Experiences). A semi-structured interview elicited detailed accounts of participant experiences during the follow-up period. Reflexive thematic analysis was employed to examine subjective experiences and contextual factors influencing outcomes. Results: An integrative summary of quantitative and qualitative findings will be presented. Three major themes were developed: (1) Life before PAT, characterized by emotional struggles and cautious hope; (2) Experiences during PAT marked by emotional shifts and the role of therapeutic support; and (3) Life after PAT, encompassing enduring changes in relationships, self-awareness, and behavior, alongside a desire for further integration. Conclusion: Findings underscore the importance of mixed-methods in capturing the nuanced, long-term impact of PAT to further understanding of mechanisms of change. Additionally, we emphasize the need for more robust theoretical models to inform integration therapies and psychedelic-informed education to equip providers and participants with clear guidelines that mitigate risks, enhance safety, and foster ongoing integration for sustained therapeutic benefit over time.
