T1  - DOES THE PHENOMENOLOGY OF THE PSILOCYBIN EXPERIENCE PREDICT LONG-TERM EFFECTS ON MOOD AND WELL-BEING IN HEALTHY VOLUNTEERS?

A1  - Palenicek T
A1  - Kluckova T
A1  - Nikolic M
A1  - Vejmola C
A1  - Tyls F
A1  - Viktorin V
A1  - Horacek J
A1  - Brunovsky M
Y1  - 2023//
Y2  - 2023//
N2  - INTRODUCTION: The mechanisms by which the psychedelic drug psilocybin, a novel rapid-acting antidepressant, improves depressive symptoms are still unclear. One candidate mechanism is the impact of the phenomenology of the psychedelic experience, with positive phenomenology hypothesised to be associated with positive long-term outcomes and fearful phenomenology hypothesised to be associated with negative long-term outcomes. The present study investigated how phenomenology influences long-term effects on mood, spirituality, and relationships with self, life, and others following single and repeated experiences in healthy volunteers. MATERIALS AND METHODS: In a placebo-controlled cross-over study, healthy volunteers participated in two consecutive arms of two sessions each of psilocybin 0.26 mg/kg orally or placebo, separated by at least 28 days. Phenomenology of the experience was assessed using the Altered State of Consciousness Scale (ASCs) and the Visual Analog Scale on the Emotional Valence of the Experience (VAS-E); long-term effects on mood, spirituality, and relationships with self, life, and others were assessed using the Persisting Effects Scale (PEQ) 28 days after each treatment (both positive and negative effects). RESULTS: 39 volunteers completed the first arm of the study and 27 continued into the second arm. There were no differences in ASC and PEQ between the first and second arms. Correlations between ASC and PEQ showed that the intensity of the experience, driven mainly by the oceanic boundlessness component, was associated with the long-term positive effect. The negative/anxious experiences on ASC and VAS-E were not associated with a negative effect on PEQ. CONCLUSIONS: Our study shows for the first time that repeated experience with psilocybin in a controlled environment can keep comparable acute as well as long-term effect on an individual's well-being, which supports rational for safe repeated use in a therapeutic context. SUPPORTED BY PROJECTS: NU21-04-00307, 20-25349S, RVO 00023752, 260648/SVV/2023, Cooperatio and PSYRES, Psychedelic Research Foundation (https://psyresfoundation.eu) Declaration of Interest Statement: TP, JH, MB and FT were involved in Compass Pathways trials with psilocybin outside of the submitted work.
