T1  - Acute Psychedelic Effects and Clinical Outcomes are Associated With Therapeutic Alliance Among Participants Receiving Psilocybin Therapy

A1  - Davis A
A1  - Levin A
A1  - Lancelotta R
A1  - Sepeda N
A1  - Gukasyan N
A1  - Nayak S
A1  - Barrett F
A1  - Griffiths R
Y1  - 2024//
Y2  - 2024//
N2  - Select Drug Category Psychedelics Topic Behavioral Pharmacology Abstract Detail Clinical - Experimental Abstract Category Original Research Aim: Research has demonstrated an association between therapeutic alliance between therapist and client and positive treatment outcomes in psychotherapy. Here we explore whether therapeutic alliance is associated with acute psilocybin effects and depression outcomes following psilocybin therapy. Methods: A randomized, waiting list-controlled trial for Major Depressive Disorder (N=24). Participants were randomized to an immediate (N=13) or delayed (N=11) treatment condition and all participants received two oral doses of psilocybin approximately 2 weeks apart (Session 1: 20 mg/70 kg; Session 2: 30 mg/70 kg), as well as psychotherapy prior to and following the psilocybin sessions. We used the bond subscale of the Working Alliance Inventory – Short Form as a measure of therapeutic alliance and examined the relationship between bond scores and peak mystical-type and insight experiences as well as depression scores (using the GRID-HAMD) post-intervention using Pearson correlations. Results: A stronger bond before the first psilocybin session was correlated with higher peak ratings of mystical-type experiences (r=.50, p<.05) and psychological insight (r=.54, p<.05) across the two psilocybin sessions. Additionally, peak ratings of mystical-type experiences (r=-.44; p<.05) and psychological insight (r=-0.75, p<.001) were correlated with depression scores at 4 weeks post-intervention. A stronger bond was also correlated with depression scores at 4 weeks (r=-.49, p<.05). A stronger bond before the first psilocybin session did not predict long term depression scores (ps>.05), but a stronger bond one week after the final psilocybin session predicted depression scores at 3-months (r=-.57, p<.01), 6-months (r=-.66, p<.001), and 12-months (r=-.71, p<.001) post-intervention. Conclusions: A stronger bond was associated with higher peak ratings of mystical-type and psychologically insightful experiences and clinical outcomes of psilocybin therapy. These findings show the importance of the therapeutic relationship in clinical trials of psilocybin therapy and provide evidence for the rationale that characteristics of (mind)set and setting impact the acute effects of psilocybin. Financial Support: This study was funded in part by a crowd-sourced funding campaign organized by Tim Ferriss; a grant from the Riverstyx Foundation; and grants from Tim Ferriss, Matt Mullenweg, Craig Nerenberg, Blake Mycoskie, and the Steven and Alexandra Cohen Foundation. Drs Davis and Levin and Mx. Lancelotta and Mr. Sepeda are supported by the Center for Psychedelic Drug Research and Education at Ohio State University.
