Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Reply by the current authors the comments made by Kelley C. O'Donnell et al.(see record 2025-59881-008), Michael P. Bogenschutz (see record 2025-59881-009),Eduardo Ekman Schenberg(see record 2025-59881-010), Michael D. Alpert et al.(see record 2025-59881-011), Garrett Marie Deckel et al.(see record 2025-59881-012),Mitch Earleywine et al. (see record 2025-59881-013) on the original article (see record 2025-59881-001). O'Donnell et al. simply contradict our assertion that psilocybin-assisted therapy "has not been shown to be a psychotherapy as normally understood." The further dismissive comments about the COMP001 study are not very relevant to the argument, but they are misleading. First, this trial was conducted in a difficult-to- treat population, so it is unsurprising that it reported smaller effects than those in highly selected major depression samples. We agree that establishing how much psychological support or psychotherapy to layer in or layer out should be decided by future clinical trials and that patient safety should be the primary concern. In conclusion, we do not pretend to have all the answers. Indeed, we still believe we need to go on defining the questions. However, premature closure cannot be a good thing, so we have not settled here for bland accommodation. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved)
