ID  - 2026-91013-013
T1  - Microdosing psilocybin as a safe and effective treatment for chronic fibromyalgia pain.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Siegel, Cassandra
A1  - Foster, Gervaisa

Y1  - 2026//
N2  - Purpose: Chronic fibromyalgia pain has proven to be one of the more challenging pain management conditions to treat, significantly impacting patients' quality of life. Current fibromyalgia treatments often fail to provide consistent pain and symptom relief. Psilocybin has re-emerged as a potential therapy for chronic pain conditions. Microdosing psilocybin could mitigate its hallucinogenic effects while preserving its anti-nociceptive, anti-inflammatory, and neuroplastic benefits. Methodology: A systematic review was conducted through an exhaustive search for articles that focused on fibromyalgia, psilocybin, effective pain management, and the safety of psilocybin for therapeutic use in the adult population. After screening 490 articles, three were selected based on relevance. Articles that focused on mental health, substance abuse, non-psilocybin pain treatments, or other chronic pain conditions were excluded. Results: Current research indicates that both macrodoses and microdoses of psilocybin provide pain relief for fibromyalgia. Macrodoses offer greater and longer-lasting pain relief than microdoses but also carry a higher risk of negative side effects. Anxiety, paranoia, and gastrointestinal distress were the most common adverse effects. Though there is a theoretical risk of serotonin syndrome and cardiotoxicity, case reports are rare, suggesting a wide safety margin for psilocybin.Implications for Practice and Research: Despite promising findings, more randomized controlled trials with standardized dosing are needed to solidify psilocybin's role in fibromyalgia treatment. Clinicians should use screening tools to exclude patients with psychosis or cardiovascular conditions. Protocols should be developed for dosing, nausea prevention, and the therapeutic use of psilocybin. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved)
