T1  - Illuminating the psychedelic experience: The relationship between psychedelics, depression, and meaning in life.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Jimenez, Bryan

Y1  - 2026//
N2  - Traditional treatments for depression, including psychotherapy and pharmacological interventions, are not effective for all individuals-particularly those with treatment-resistant depression. As interest in alternative modalities grows, psychedelics such as psilocybin have gained attention for their potential to reduce depressive symptoms and enhance meaning in life. This study examined the relationship between psilocybin use and scores on depression and meaning-related measures in individuals with treatment-resistant depression. Participants (N = 16) completed three standardized measures: the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HDRS), the Meaning in Life Questionnaire (MLQ), and the Personal Meaning Profile-Brief (PMP-B). Participants were grouped based on whether they had used psilocybin within the past 5 years. The primary researcher conducted independent samples t-tests, a Pearson correlation matrix, and a multivariate analysis of variance (MANOVA). No statistically significant differences were found between psilocybin users and non-users on HDRS (p = 0.115), MLQ (p = 0.885), or PMP-B (p = 0.629) scores. However, the results indicated that psilocybin users had slightly lower depression scores (average = 18.57, standard deviation = 6.07) and somewhat higher meaning-in-life scores (average = 28.43, standard deviation = 4.52) compared to non-users (depression average = 19.50, standard deviation = 7.78; meaning-in-life average = 27.00, standard deviation = 9.90). The MANOVA revealed a moderate multivariate effect size (eta2 = 0.254), though the results did not reach significance. These preliminary findings highlight the continued need for research using larger, more balanced samples and more comprehensive designs to explore how psychedelic experiences may influence depression, personal meaning, and existential well-being. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
