T1  - Psilocybin-induced structural plasticity and the role of 5-HT2A receptors.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Liao, Clara

Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Serotonergic psychedelics are hallucinogenic compounds that produce an altered state of consciousness and perception. Among these, psilocybin has emerged as a promising new avenue in the treatment of neuropsychiatric disorders. Early clinical trials earned psilocybin a 'breakthrough therapy' designation from the FDA for major depressive disorder in 2019. And yet while the therapeutic effects are promising, the neurobiological mechanisms through which psilocybin affects the brain remain poorly understood. This dissertation aims to provide cellular-level insights that elucidate key biological players mediating psilocybin's therapeutic effects and highlight structural plasticity as a common neural mechanism shared by fast-acting antidepressants. Preclinical studies have shown that fast-acting antidepressants such as ketamine promote structural remodeling of dendritic spines. Given that deficits in structural plasticity in the prefrontal cortex are a physiological hallmark linked to symptoms of depression in humans, the rapid antidepressant response is thought to arise from the induction of persistent neural adaptation that reverses pathophysiological deficits present in depression. While psychedelics also appear to promote plasticity, it remains unclear whether the therapeutic effects are directly tied to changes in structural neural plasticity, and whether the hallucinogenic properties can be decoupled from their therapeutic benefits. This dissertation will present three independent studies that reveal the critical role of structural plasticity in psilocybin's action and will begin to untangle the circuit and molecular mechanisms underlying the changes in both structural plasticity and behavior. In Chapter 1, we demonstrate that a single dose of psilocybin induces a sustained increase in structural plasticity and excitatory transmission in vivo in mouse frontal cortex. Using chronic two-photon microscopy, we longitudinally image the apical dendritic spines of layer 5 pyramidal neurons in the mouse medial frontal cortex. We find that a single dose of psilocybin leads to ~10% increases in dendritic spine size and density, driven by an elevated spine formation rate. This structural remodeling occurs rapidly, within 24 hours, and persists for at least one month. Additionally, the study provides evidence that psilocybin can mitigate maladaptive behaviors induced by uncontrollable stress in mice. Collectively, these results demonstrate that psilocybin-evoked synaptic rewiring in the frontal cortex is both fast and enduring, potentially providing a structural trace for long-term integration of experiences and lasting therapeutic effects. A deeper understanding of psilocybin's actions hinges on pinpointing the circuit-level and molecular targets that drive changes in structural plasticity and behavior. In Chapter 2, we first investigate the impact of psilocybin on the two main subtypes of pyramidal cells in the mouse medial frontal cortex. We find that a single dose of psilocybin has differential effects on the density and size of dendritic spines in pyramidal tract and intratelencephalic neurons. Behaviorally, silencing pyramidal tract neurons eliminates psilocybin's ability to ameliorate stress-related phenotypes, whereas silencing intratelencephalic neurons has no detectable effect. Next, levering a conditional knockout mouse line, we observe that targeted knockout of 5-HT2A receptors abolishes psilocybin's effects on stress-related behavior and structural plasticity. Collectively, these results highlight the critical roles of a specific pyramidal cell type and the 5- HT2A receptor in the medial frontal cortex in mediating the long-term effects of psilocybin. In Chapter 3, we broaden our focus towards how dendritic plasticity may play a crucial role in various rapid-acting depression treatments by restoring excitatory synaptic function. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
