Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Comments on an article by SA. Wilke et al. (see record 2022-27426-001). Wilke et al. advances our mechanistic understanding of PFC dopamine D2Rs and depression in many ways, with a key finding being identification of a circuit-level convergence for depression-related manipulations-correlated activity of deep-layer projection neurons in PFC-that could explain some of the effects of dopamine on mood and affect. A related critical finding was that acute ketamine and chronic fluoxetine also modulated correlated PFC activity, which suggests the possibility that this biomarker may be a common feature of disparate antidepressants including classic psychedelics such as psilocybin, which at least in human basal ganglia are powerful dopamine releasers. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
